-- title:
--   Theorem 4.2(a), p.21 — optimal stopping under payoff symmetry (corrected: $\underline f(\tau^*,r^R)$ for $a_1$; $u_2^{**}$)
-- statement:
--   Assume the standing assumptions and payoff symmetry $u_{01}=u_{10}$, and let $(\underline\pi,\overline\pi,\tilde z)$ be the indifference point, in a valid signal model. "$a_i$ is optimal on stopping at $t$" means that the Gilboa–Schmeidler utility of $a_i$ at $(t,Z_t)$ attains $X(t,Z_t)$ (for $a_2$: $X=u_2$).
--
--   **(a.i)** Let $(r^l_1,r^l_2)$ and $(r^R_1,r^R_2)$ solve (31) and (30), and $r^l_1\le\overline\pi$. Let
--   $$\tau_0=\min\{t\ge0:Z_t\le\overline f(t,r^l_2)\}=\min\{t\ge0:\overline m_t\le r^l_2\},\qquad \tau_1=\min\{t\ge0:Z_t\ge\underline f(t,r^R_2)\}=\min\{t\ge0:\underline m_t\ge r^R_2\},$$
--   $$\tau_2=\min\{t\ge0:\overline f(t,r^l_1)\le Z_t\le\underline f(t,r^R_1)\}=\min\{t\ge0:\overline m_t\ge r^l_1,\ \underline m_t\le r^R_1\}.$$
--   Then $\tau^*=\min\{\tau_0,\tau_1,\tau_2\}$ is optimal in (9); if $\tau^*=\tau_i<\infty$, $a_i$ is optimal on stopping; and if $\underline\pi=\underline m_0$ and $\overline\pi=\overline m_0$ (ex ante indifference), then $\tau^*=0$ and $a_2$ is chosen.
--
--   **(a.ii)** Let $(r^R,r^l)$ solve (32) and $u_2<u_2^{**}$. Then
--   $$\tau^*=\min\{t\ge0:Z_t\le\overline f(t,r^l)\text{ or }Z_t\ge\underline f(t,r^R)\}=\min\{t\ge0:\overline m_t\le r^l\text{ or }\underline m_t\ge r^R\}$$
--   is optimal in (9); on stopping, $a_0$ is optimal if $Z_{\tau^*}\le\overline f(\tau^*,r^l)$ (equivalently $\overline m_{\tau^*}\le r^l$), $a_1$ is optimal if $Z_{\tau^*}\ge\underline f(\tau^*,r^R)$ (equivalently $\underline m_{\tau^*}\ge r^R$), and $a_2$ is never optimal.
--
--   This is the paper's general solution of the learning problem under payoff symmetry, of which Theorem 3.1 is the Ellsberg case.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: the augmented filtration $\{\mathcal G_t\}$ generated by $Z$ → any filtration $\mathbb G$ to which $Z$ is adapted and under which $Z/\sigma$ is a $(\mathbb G,P_0)$-Brownian motion; reason: the paper's filtration, with an augmentation that keeps its p.6 assertion true, is one of them, so the statement specializes to it; stopping times and density generators range over the larger class
--   - encoding: $P^\eta$ given by its density $dP^\eta/dP_0|_{\mathcal G_t}=\exp\{-\int_0^t\eta_s^2ds-\int_0^t\eta_sdW_s\}$ (printed without the factor $\tfrac12$) → the priors $Q$ with a density generator $\eta\in\Xi$, equivalent to $P_0$ on every $\mathbb G_t$, under which $W^\eta=Z/\sigma+\int_0^\cdot\eta_sds$ is a $(\mathbb G,Q)$-Brownian motion; reason: Girsanov characterization of $P^\eta$, since Mathlib has no Itô integral; for the paper's filtration it determines $Q$ on every $\mathcal G_t$, which is all the objective (9) depends on
--   - reading: §2: "each $\eta\in\Xi$ defines a probability measure ... $P^\eta$" (by Girsanov's theorem) → a standing property of the signal model: every bounded progressively measurable $\eta$ yields such a $Q$; reason: the paper asserts this as a consequence of Girsanov's theorem, which Mathlib lacks; it holds on the canonical space of continuous paths with Wiener measure and the natural filtration (the worst-case-prior milestone asserts that a valid model exists), and it fails on a filtration augmented by all null sets of $\mathbb G_\infty$, on which $P^\eta$ is singular to $P_0$
--   - encoding: $\eta_t(\omega)\in\Xi_t(\omega)$ for $dt\otimes dP_0$-a.e. $(t,\omega)$ → for every $(t,\omega)$; reason: clamping an a.e. selection to the interval $\Xi_t(\omega)$ gives a progressively measurable selection everywhere with the same $P^\eta$; the set of priors is unchanged
--   - encoding: posteriors $\underline m_t,\overline m_t$ by Bayesian updating (Liptser–Shiryaev, Theorem 9.1) → the closed form (12)–(13), as a function of $(t,z)$; reason: the paper's own formula; same objects
--   - generalization: a trajectory $(\tilde z_t)_{t\ge0}$ → a function $\tilde z:\mathbb R\to\mathbb R$ with the defining identities for every real $t$; reason: the closed forms extend to all real $t$; the paper's trajectory is the restriction to $t\ge0$, and uniqueness on $\mathbb R$ implies uniqueness on $[0,\infty)$
--   - encoding: stopping times with values in $\mathbb R_+$ → stopping times in $[0,\infty]$ (`WithTop ℝ≥0`); on $\{\tau=\infty\}$ the payoff is evaluated at time $0$; reason: for $\tau\in\Gamma$ the set $\{\tau=\infty\}$ is null under every prior, so the objective is unchanged
--   - encoding: $\min\{t\ge0:\dots\}$ → the first hitting time `hittingAfter` of a closed set, $=\infty$ if it is never hit; reason: with continuous paths the minimum is attained when the set is hit; paths that never hit are null under every prior for the stopping times of the paper
--   - reading: "the optimal stopping time $\tau^*$ is given by ..." → the given $\tau^*$ is optimal in (9); reason: the proofs establish exactly this ("$\tau^*$ is the optimal stopping time", p.28); uniqueness is neither claimed nor proved
--   - reading: "$a_i$ is optimal on stopping", "$a_2$ is chosen", "$a_2$ is never optimal" → the Gilboa–Schmeidler utility of $a_i$ at $(\tau^*,Z_{\tau^*})$ equals $X$; $X=u_2$; $u_2<X$, at every $\omega$ with $\tau^*(\omega)<\infty$; reason: direct meaning of (16); stated pathwise, which is stronger than almost surely
--   - correction: (a.ii): "$a_1$ is optimal if $Z_{\tau^*}\ge\overline f(\tau^*,r^R)$" → $a_1$ is optimal if $Z_{\tau^*}\ge\underline f(\tau^*,r^R)$; reason: counterexample with all assumptions: $\theta_0=-1$, $\theta_1=1$, $\sigma=1$, $c=2$, $\underline m_0=0.2$, $\overline m_0=0.8$, $u_{00}=u_{11}=1$, $u_{01}=u_{10}=0$, $u_2=0<u_2^{**}\approx0.214$; then $\tilde z\equiv0$, $r^R\approx0.228$, $r^l\approx0.772$, and a path stopped at the lower boundary $Z=\overline f(\tau^*,r^l)\approx-0.084\ge\overline f(\tau^*,r^R)\approx-1.302$ has $\overline m=0.772<\overline\pi$, where $a_0$ (utility $0.228$) beats $a_1$ (utility $\approx0.174$); the stopping rule and the posterior form on the same page use $\underline f$
--   - generalization: case (a.ii): $r_1^l>\overline\pi$ → $u_2<u_2^{**}$; reason: by (37) the two are equivalent whenever (31) has a solution; $u_2<u_2^{**}$ also covers the case where (31) has none, and the paper's proof of (a.ii) uses only $u_2<u_2^{**}$
--   - correction: (34): $u_2^{**}=\tfrac{\hat c}{2}[\cdots]+\tfrac{u_{00}-u_{01}}{2}$ → $u_2^{**}=\tfrac{\hat c}{2}[\cdots]+\tfrac{u_{00}+u_{01}}{2}$; reason: the printed value contradicts (37) and the paper's identity $u_2^{**}=g_1(\overline\pi;C_1,C_2)$ (p.26): in the Ellsberg instance $\alpha=1/8$, $c=0.01$, $\sigma=1$, $\epsilon=0.04$ (all assumptions hold) the printed $u_2^{**}\approx0.126<u_2=1/2$ while $r_1^l=\hat r\approx0.5244>\overline\pi=0.52$; the corrected value $\approx0.5011$ equals $g_1(\overline\pi)$ and $v_0$ of (26)
-- source:
--   Epstein & Ji, Optimal Learning under Robustness and Time-Consistency, arXiv:1708.01890v2, Theorem 4.2(a), p.21

import Definitions.Def_EllsbergLearning_Model
import Definitions.Def_EllsbergLearning_CriticalValues

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory Set
open scoped NNReal ENNReal

namespace EllsbergLearning

/-- Theorem 4.2(a) (Epstein & Ji, arXiv:1708.01890v2, p.21). Assume the standing assumptions of
§3.1 and payoff symmetry (`u₀₁ = u₁₀`); let `(π̲, π̄, z̃)` be the indifference point. "`aᵢ` is
optimal on stopping at `t`" means that the Gilboa–Schmeidler utility of `aᵢ` at `(t, Z_t)` attains
the payoff `X(t, Z_t)` of (16) (for `a₂`: `X = u₂`).

(a.i) Let `(r₁ˡ, r₂ˡ)` and `(r₁ᴿ, r₂ᴿ)` solve (31) and (30), and `r₁ˡ ≤ π̄`. With
`τ₀ = min{t ≥ 0 : Z_t ≤ f̄(t, r₂ˡ)} = min{t ≥ 0 : m̄_t ≤ r₂ˡ}`,
`τ₁ = min{t ≥ 0 : Z_t ≥ f̲(t, r₂ᴿ)} = min{t ≥ 0 : m̲_t ≥ r₂ᴿ}`,
`τ₂ = min{t ≥ 0 : f̄(t, r₁ˡ) ≤ Z_t ≤ f̲(t, r₁ᴿ)} = min{t ≥ 0 : m̄_t ≥ r₁ˡ and m̲_t ≤ r₁ᴿ}`
(each equality holding), `τ* = min{τ₀, τ₁, τ₂}` is optimal in (9); if `τ* = τᵢ < ∞` then `aᵢ` is
optimal on stopping; and if `π̲ = m̲₀` and `π̄ = m̄₀`, then `τ* = 0` and `a₂` is chosen
(`X(0, 0) = u₂`).

(a.ii) Let `(rᴿ, rˡ)` solve (32) and `u₂ < u₂**`. Then
`τ* = min{t ≥ 0 : Z_t ≤ f̄(t, rˡ) or Z_t ≥ f̲(t, rᴿ)} = min{t ≥ 0 : m̄_t ≤ rˡ or m̲_t ≥ rᴿ}` is
optimal in (9); on stopping (`τ* = t < ∞`), `a₀` is optimal if `Z_{τ*} ≤ f̄(τ*, rˡ)`
(equivalently `m̄_{τ*} ≤ rˡ`), `a₁` is optimal if `Z_{τ*} ≥ f̲(τ*, rᴿ)` (equivalently
`m̲_{τ*} ≥ rᴿ`), and `a₂` is never optimal (`u₂ < X`).

**Formalization Note.** (a.ii)'s hypothesis is printed "`r₁ˡ > π̄`"; it is stated as
`u₂ < u₂**`, which is equivalent to it whenever (31) has a solution (`lemma_4_1_symmetric`) and
also covers the case where (31) has none. The page prints "`a₁` is optimal if
`Z_{τ*} ≥ f̄(τ*, rᴿ)`"; the stopping rule and the equivalent posterior form use `f̲`. Stopping
times are first hitting times (`hittingAfter`) of closed sets, so "min" is attained on `{τ < ∞}`. -/
theorem theorem_4_2_a (p : Params) (hp : p.Valid) (hsym : p.PayoffSymmetry) (πL πU : ℝ)
    (zt : ℝ → ℝ) (hind : p.IsIndifference πL πU zt)
    {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (M : SignalModel Ω) (hM : M.IsValid p.σ) :
    let path : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ × ℝ := fun s ω => ((s : ℝ), M.Z s ω)
    (∀ r1l r2l r1R r2R : ℝ, p.Solves31 r1l r2l → p.Solves30 r1R r2R → r1l ≤ πU →
      let τ0 := hittingAfter path {q | q.2 ≤ p.fUp q.1 r2l} 0
      let τ1 := hittingAfter path {q | p.fLo q.1 r2R ≤ q.2} 0
      let τ2 := hittingAfter path {q | p.fUp q.1 r1l ≤ q.2 ∧ q.2 ≤ p.fLo q.1 r1R} 0
      let τ : Ω → WithTop ℝ≥0 := fun ω => min (τ0 ω) (min (τ1 ω) (τ2 ω))
      τ0 = hittingAfter path {q | p.mUp q.1 q.2 ≤ r2l} 0 ∧
      τ1 = hittingAfter path {q | r2R ≤ p.mLo q.1 q.2} 0 ∧
      τ2 = hittingAfter path {q | r1l ≤ p.mUp q.1 q.2 ∧ p.mLo q.1 q.2 ≤ r1R} 0 ∧
      M.IsOptimal p τ ∧
      (∀ (ω : Ω) (t : ℝ≥0), τ ω = t →
        (τ0 ω = t → p.gs 0 t (M.Z t ω) = p.X t (M.Z t ω)) ∧
        (τ1 ω = t → p.gs 1 t (M.Z t ω) = p.X t (M.Z t ω)) ∧
        (τ2 ω = t → p.u₂ = p.X t (M.Z t ω))) ∧
      (πL = p.mLo0 → πU = p.mUp0 → (∀ ω, τ ω = 0) ∧ p.X 0 0 = p.u₂)) ∧
    (∀ rR rl : ℝ, p.Solves32 πL πU rR rl → p.u₂ < p.u2StarStar πU rl →
      let τ := hittingAfter path {q | q.2 ≤ p.fUp q.1 rl ∨ p.fLo q.1 rR ≤ q.2} 0
      τ = hittingAfter path {q | p.mUp q.1 q.2 ≤ rl ∨ rR ≤ p.mLo q.1 q.2} 0 ∧
      M.IsOptimal p τ ∧
      ∀ (ω : Ω) (t : ℝ≥0), τ ω = t →
        (M.Z t ω ≤ p.fUp t rl ↔ p.mUp t (M.Z t ω) ≤ rl) ∧
        (M.Z t ω ≤ p.fUp t rl → p.gs 0 t (M.Z t ω) = p.X t (M.Z t ω)) ∧
        (p.fLo t rR ≤ M.Z t ω ↔ rR ≤ p.mLo t (M.Z t ω)) ∧
        (p.fLo t rR ≤ M.Z t ω → p.gs 1 t (M.Z t ω) = p.X t (M.Z t ω)) ∧
        p.u₂ < p.X t (M.Z t ω)) := by sorry

end EllsbergLearning
